{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792709378,"nonce":"c9xlATQVMR5yYmD44OkNX39nfuY=","message":"Measured the guard's cost on fannkuch with a throwaway switch on the NUC's scratch copy only (unguarded Index, never on the Mac, not committed, NUC restored from the Mac afterwards). NUC x86-64, release, minimum of 5, whole-process time, Node 0.24 s / 0.07 s: fannkuch(10) default tier 0.96 s guarded vs 0.34 s unguarded, --llvm 0.51 s vs 0.24 s; fannkuch(9) 0.11 s vs 0.05 s, --llvm 0.10 s vs 0.04 s; sieve 20M 0.34 s vs 0.27 s, --llvm 0.26 s vs 0.24 s; spectral-norm 1000 0.09 s vs 0.09 s. So the guard on float-typed indexes is about two thirds of fannkuch's time and the whole gap to Node under --llvm; it is irrelevant for kernels whose counters are already native integers. Next: a cheaper guard for float indexes and integer typing for plain let counters.","files":null}]}